  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "A homeowner is tracking the rate at which electricity is being used in her house. The rate $r(t)$ at which electricity is consumed is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=2{t}^{2}-8t+10,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in kilowatts, and $0<t<6$ is the time in hours since $9\\,\\text{am}.$\n\nBetween $t=0$ and $t=3$ hours,\n{{field-1}} kilowatt-hours were consumed.",
  "worked_solution": "We're given the **rate of change** of electricity consumption $r(t).$ To find the **net amount** of electricity consumed by the house between the times $t=0$ and $t=3,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2{t}^{2}-8t+10)\\,\\text{d}t & ={(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{8}{2}{t}^{2}+10t)|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+10t)|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}(3)^{3}-4(3)^{2}+10(3))-(0-0+0) \\\\  & =18-36+30 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, between $t=0$ and $t=3$ hours, $12$ kilowatt-hours were consumed.",
